          "text": "Dear Colleague,\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nThe patient has generalized epilepsy with typical absence seizures, beginning at 69 years of age. The current seizure frequency is approximately three seizures per month. The epilepsy is drug-resistant and categorized as structural in etiology. MRI demonstrates focal cortical dysplasia.\n\nCurrent Antiseizure Medications:\n- Topiramate\n- Perampanel\n- Carbamazepine\n- Zonisamide\n\nSeizure Burden Summary:\nTypical absence seizures occurring approximately three times per month despite the current antiseizure medication regimen.\n\nAssessment:\nDrug-resistant generalized epilepsy with typical absence seizures and onset at age 69. Structural etiology is documented, with focal cortical dysplasia on MRI.\n\nPlan:\nContinue the current antiseizure medications: topiramate, perampanel, carbamazepine, and zonisamide. Continue tracking seizure frequency.\n\nSincerely,  \nEpilepsy Clinic",
